{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"communal-brazen-veery","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# Outbound status never transitions on Data Center — `update_issue` has no status routing\n\n## Symptom\n\nLive J11 cell `test_outbound_mutation_round_trips[04-transition-status]` fails against the\nDockerized Jira Data Center harness with:\n\n    outbound status did not reach DC: fields.status.name is 'To Do'\n\nThe pass exits 0 with no traceback. A local `open -> in_progress` transition is reconciled as\n\"applied\" and the DC issue's status never changes.\n\n## This is NOT covered by 7f93\n\nBug 7f93 taught `JiraDataCenterTransport.transition_issue_by_name` to resolve a destination\nSTATUS name (`\"In Progress\"`) to the transition that reaches it (`Start Progress`). That fixed\nthe INBOUND cell, whose setup helper `_in_status` calls `transition_issue_by_name` DIRECTLY.\n\n**The outbound apply path never calls `transition_issue_by_name` at all.** 7f93 cannot fix it.\n\n## Root cause, traced end to end\n\n1. `adapters/jira_family/outbound_mapper.py:81-82` maps local `in_progress` to the Jira\n   workflow-state name via `LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA`, emitting `fields[\"status\"] = \"In Progress\"`.\n2. `dispatch_apply_phases.py:41` — `_OUTBOUND_BATCH_ALLOWLIST` CONTAINS `\"status\"`, so\n   `_update_one_filter_fields` (`dispatch_apply_phases.py:130-139`) keeps it.\n3. `dispatch_one.py:665` (`_update_one_scalar_update`) forwards the whole allowlisted dict as\n   `client.update_issue(issue_key, **fields)`.\n4. `client` is the configured backend's transport — `applier.py:591` calls `_load_acli()`, which\n   is `select_backend(load_config()).transport` (`applier.py:221-231`). On this deployment that\n   is `JiraDataCenterTransport`.\n5. **On Cloud**, `adapters/jira/acli.py:170` pops `status` out of the kwargs and\n   `acli.py:182-183` routes it to `transition_issue` -> `transition_issue_by_name`. The\n   status→transition translation lives IN THE ACLI TRANSPORT, not in the shared dispatch path.\n6. **On Data Center**, `adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py:248-255` pops ONLY `assignee` and\n   puts everything else into `issue.update(fields=kwargs)` — a REST field EDIT\n   (`PUT /rest/api/2/issue/<key>`). `status` is not an editable field in Jira; it is reachable\n   only through a transition. Jira rejects it, the error is soft-failed, and the status stays put.\n\nSo the DC transport is MISSING the status→transition routing its Cloud sibling has. The\ntranslation was implemented once, per-transport, and the second transport did not get it — the\nsame shape as bug 3f04 (outbound links re-added to one path only).\n\n## Reproduction (no live Jira)\n\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_dc_outbound_status_routing_5200.py`\n\n* `test_the_dc_transport_edits_status_as_a_field_instead_of_transitioning` — GREEN today, pins\n  the defect: `update_issue(\"DIG-1\", status=\"In Progress\")` produces\n  `edited_fields == [{\"status\": \"In Progress\"}]`, `transitions_probed == []`,\n  `transitioned == []`. No transitions probe happens at all.\n* `test_an_outbound_status_should_dispatch_the_transition_that_reaches_it` — `xfail(strict=True)`,\n  asserts the required behaviour (`transitioned == [(\"DIG-1\", \"11\")]`, i.e. `Start Progress`).\n  Verified with `--runxfail` that it fails naming the property:\n  `AssertionError: the outbound status 'In Progress' must resolve to transition 'Start Progress'\n  (id 11) and be dispatched; got transitioned=[] and edited_fields=[{'status': 'In Progress'}]`.\n  `strict=True` so the build fails when the bug is fixed, forcing the marker off.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n1. `JiraDataCenterTransport.update_issue` pops `status` out of the kwargs and routes it through\n   `self.transition_issue_by_name(remote_id, status)` — which since 7f93 already resolves either\n   a transition name or a destination status name — and never submits `status` through\n   `issue.update(fields=...)`.\n2. The remaining allowlisted fields still go through the field edit in the same call, and the\n   `assignee` routing is unchanged.\n3. `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_dc_outbound_status_routing_5200.py`'s `xfail(strict=True)`\n   marker is REMOVED and the test passes; the characterization test that pins the defect is\n   inverted (or deleted) with the reason recorded in its docstring — not silently relaxed.\n4. A transition that Jira rejects (illegal from the current workflow state) must remain\n   non-fatal, matching the existing `_is_illegal_transition_400` comment-fallback contract, and\n   must be OBSERVABLE (bridge alert), not silent.\n5. Live evidence: `test_outbound_mutation_round_trips[04-transition-status]` green on a real DC\n   harness run.","id":"d067-5d2f-bcee-46ae","parent_id":"e369-a449-4773-48fb","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"DC outbound status is submitted as a field edit, never a transition — update_issue has no status routing"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785691320624912001,"uuid":"c7437f7a-e4dc-4fc8-8d67-74aa010ca903"}